{"name":"Jambalaya","hex":"#f7b572","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jambalaya","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various, rice-based dishes common in Louisiana, Cajun, and Creole cuisine, most often with shrimp, oysters, chicken, or ham."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jambalaya"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jambalaya","blocks":[{"text":"Jambalaya is a savory rice dish that developed in the U.S. state of Louisiana fusing together African, Spanish, and French influences, consisting mainly of meat and/or seafood, and vegetables mixed with rice and spices. West Africans and Spanish people each had versions of jambalaya in their respective countries. The tomato, a mainstay ingredient of the dish, was introduced to West Africans by the French and was subsequently incorporated into their one-pot rice dishes. Spanish people made paella, which is also a one-pot rice dish cooked with meats and vegetables. These styles of cuisines blended in Louisiana and resulted in cultural and regional variations of the dish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Homemade_Jambalaya.JPG/330px-Homemade_Jambalaya.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jambalaya","wikidata":"Q1364509"},{"title":"Jambalaya (On the Bayou)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Jambalaya (On the Bayou)\" is a song written and recorded by American country music singer Hank Williams that was first released in July 1952. It is Williams' most recorded song. Named for a Creole and Cajun dish, jambalaya, it spawned numerous recordings and has since achieved popularity in several different music genres."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jambalaya_(On_the_Bayou)","wikidata":"Q1413414"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg/500px-JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg/1280px-JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg/500px-JOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"West African jollof rice is cooked with tomatoes and poultry. Some historians suggest this dish influenced the jambalaya.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJOLLOF_RICE_WITH_CHICKEN.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg/500px-01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg/1280px-01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg/500px-01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Some historians suggest Valenciana paella influenced jambalaya.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A01_Paella_Valenciana_original.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Thi%C3%A9boudi%C3%A8ne_Boukhonk_with_tamarind.jpg/500px-Thi%C3%A9boudi%C3%A8ne_Boukhonk_with_tamarind.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Thi%C3%A9boudi%C3%A8ne_Boukhonk_with_tamarind.jpg/500px-Thi%C3%A9boudi%C3%A8ne_Boukhonk_with_tamarind.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Author Ibraham Seck suggests that jambalaya has origins in Senegalese cuisine.","link":"https:/